\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi720\li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid9663034 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs22\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs22\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 Chapter 7 \endash  At Blade-Point
\par 
\par The house was one of those finer city apartments people would spend the summers in. Small for an estate, but larger than most common buildings in the city, with its own cozy grounds that wrapped around the left side and back lawn.
 White-washed brick, with a well-kept clay roof and patterned walkways through the front garden, pruned-back rose bushes and evergreen shrubs. A fence and gate to keep out the rabble, but only four foot high. Quaint, comfortable, and moneyed\'85
 but not ostentatiously so.
\par I never would have looked twice at it, searching the city. There were dozens of others just like it, and the family name had no connection to the case I could have ever found on my own. It was amazing what difference the right information could make.

\par Malachi had a case slung over his shoulder that I\rquote d seen him pull out of a lockbox on his way out the door. I knew what it was, because I\rquote d been trained on them too, extensively. I didn\rquote t have one of my own. 
They were almost exclusively a weapon of death, meant to kill or maim, with precision. They were each finely-tuned, expensive, personalized, and I didn\rquote t warrant one being made for me, yet.
\par He unstrapped the collapsible crossbow from inside its case, and released the safety, triggering the mechanism to unfold. He checked the tension once, then loaded a bolt from the clip, staring down the crosshairs a moment as he spoke to me.
\par \'93I have no spare, so you\rquote ll need to rely on your blade if it comes to it. Tell me you\rquote re confident in your skills, and I\rquote ll believe you.\'94 
\par \'93I\rquote m confident I can best her in hand-to-hand combat before I\rquote ll need to use steel,\'94 I said. 
\par He glanced at me. \'93If she draws a weapon of her own-\'93
\par \'93I\rquote ll do what I must,\'94 I promised.
\par \'93Good man,\'94 he affirmed, collapsing the crossbow again with the bolt safely stowed in the chamber. He hooked it to his harness and slung it over his shoulder. \'93We\rquote ll need to outfit you with a leather gambeson, if you\rquote 
re going to do work like this. Cloth won\rquote t cut it. Also, I\rquote d meant to ask you. Your hand\'85\'94
\par \'93I\rquote m a south paw,\'94 I assured him. 
\par \'93Ah, I think I remember that,\'94 he nodded. \'93That\rquote s a nasty surprise, if you\rquote re any good. Having to reverse your guard is always tricky. Could catch a man unawares in the first few beats, if he\rquote s not paying attention.\'94

\par \'93Our Senior Monk, Brother Dolus,\'94 I swallowed, \'93seemed to think it would be a benefit to me in the future.\'94
\par \'93That\rquote s foresight,\'94 Malachi nodded. \'93Most Abbeys would train out a quirk like that. Your Senior Monk\rquote s a canny one, bucking tradition.\'94 He glanced down at my hand, and my missing finger. \'93
Although I suppose in your case, it was advisable regardless.\'94
\par I only nodded. The pinky I was missing may or may not have ever had an appreciable effect on my grip on my right hand, but the time I\rquote d spent healing from losing it and the phantom pains it still afflicted me with certainly did.
\par \'93Have you ever affected an arrest?\'94 he asked me, testing the gate first and finding it locked, before putting a paw up between the prongs of the fence and easily vaulting himself up over it.
\par I tried to stifle my incredulity at the brazen, casual disregard he was showing. The idea of violating the sanctity of an estate\rquote 
s grounds- even a small one like this, couched in between many other buildings rather than on sprawling acreage- when we could just }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid5317331 wait}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  for the staff to come out to greet us. There was even a bell\'85
\par \'85 but then, we\rquote d lose the element of surprise. And if I\rquote d learned one thing from my failures in the past by now, it\rquote s that you didn\rquote t wait to catch up to Darcy. If we gave her }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid5317331 any}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  warning, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid5317331 any}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  amount of time, and she was truly here? We\rquote d lose her. Again. Or worse, she\rquote d lay a trap for us like she had at the carriage house. Our prey was too clever to let propriety get in our way.
\par And if we were wrong, Malachi was a dyed-in-the-wool Inquisitor. The real thing. One of the few Templar in the country who was permitted to penetrate, infiltrate and a
ct with almost total impunity in Pedigree society. The family could bring it up with the Ministry if they took offense, but most wouldn\rquote 
t dare. If word got out that your bloodline was being looked into by the Inquisition, it could mean utter defamation in 
the court of public opinion, and banishment from high society events, lest the stain of rumors rub off on the hosts. Having a House Templar was one thing. An Inquisitor paying you a visit meant something had gone }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid6232711 very}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid6232711 wrong}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 
, and no one wanted to be associated with that kind of scandal, or earn the ire of the Ministry itself.
\par \'93Brother Isidor,\'94 he called back to me.
\par I blinked rapidly, then with a jolt, realized I hadn\rquote t yet moved to follow him. I found the same purchase on the meager fence he had, and leapt it as well, landing behind him. \'93I\rquote m sorry, Inquisitor.\'94
\par \'93Malachi, please,\'94 he corrected me. \'93Or \lquote Brother,\rquote  if you must. You didn\rquote t answer me.\'94
\par \'93Oh,\'94 I stammered. \'93I\rquote m sorry,\'94 I said again, wincing at my own repetition. \'93No, I haven\rquote t\'85 I mean. I tried. Not the first time, because I didn\rquote 
t realize then who she was, but the second. But you know how that went, so ultimately\'85 no. Not successfully.\'94
\par \'93It\rquote s just as well,\'94 he assured me. \'93By the letter of the law, you aren\rquote t supposed to. You\rquote re permitted to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid6963149 investigate}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  and if you ended up bringing her in, I don\rquote t think anyone would take you to task over it. Better it be done by me. As of two weeks ago, I am officially the main agent assigned
 with arrest and interrogation privileges for this case. Have no doubt,\'94 he promised, \'93I\rquote ll defend our actions any way it happens to go down. You do what you must. If she\rquote s even here, that is\'85\'94
\par I stepped up beside him on the cobbled walkway before the main entrance, two hardwood doors with heavy brass door knockers.
\par \'93Interrogation?\'94 I asked, uncertainly. \'93Why? It\rquote s not in doubt that we have the right person. She didn\rquote t even deny it when I spoke to her.\'94  
\par \'93To locate the relic,\'94 he explained, and I instantly felt idiotic. \'93You\rquote ve got quite the head for hunting down criminals, Brother Isidor, but I excel in information retrieval, often from unwilling subjects. It\rquote 
s why they called me away from the border lands for this case in particular.\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid12722870 Torture}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 , my mind supplanted. It was an unfortunate necessity of Inquisition that I\rquote d not b
een trained in, yet. The key was keeping your subject alive, while convincing them through psychological\'85 and often physical\'85 means to part with what they knew. Earnestly, it was work I\rquote d been hoping I\rquote 
d never have to take part in, but that was probably unrealistic. He\rquote d never talked about it in detail, but even Father Helstrom had told me he\rquote d had to question people\'85 rigorously\'85 on more than one occasion.
\par \'93Try not to kill her,\'94 he said, checking his stowed blade, his gaze wandering around the edge of the building to the abutting garden. The back of the lot was ringed in a taller brick wall that separated it from the yard on the other side. \'93
If you happen to run her down.\'94
\par \'93We\rquote ll be together, won\rquote t we?\'94 I asked, confused. \'93You said you wanted to affect the arrest. I have no issue with that, but shouldn\rquote t we stick together?\'94
\par I preferred the thought, truth be told. I\rquote d failed twice now, and I was tired, worn-down and still ailing, somewhat. I wanted to catch her obviously, but if my Senior Brother was the one who did the physical catching, I wouldn\rquote 
t feel cheated. It didn\rquote t matter to me who did it.
\par \'93Actually, I want you out here watching the exits while I talk to the family,\'94 he explained. \'93I have the authority, and moreover the necessary patents to smooth things along. And she\rquote s probably }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid6232711 in}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  the house, if she\rquote s here at all. Best we not reveal our whole hand at once, yes? If she tries to run again, I want her caught unawares. I
\rquote d rather they not know there are two of us here.\'94
\par \'93Understood,\'94 I nodded crisply. Everything he was saying made sense, and while I desperately wanted to see and talk to her again, if we }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid15346217 caught}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  her this time, I\rquote d have every chance.
\par And maybe it was better if someone disconnected from all of our previous interactions was the one to manhandle her. Besides, Malachi had the patents. I didn\rquote t. It could be a legal issue.
\par \'93Go around back before I even knock,\'94 he said. \'93I don\rquote t want them seeing you, in case they\rquote ve some kind of signal or warning worked out.\'94
\par I nodded, quietly amazed at his thinking. Everything he was recommending was common sense, seemingly. Logical reasoning, that\rquote s all it was, but in his case based on what had likely amounted to a decade or more
 of experience doing this kind of work. He looked to be a decade or so older than me, at least. And I remembered him now, close to my current age or thereabouts, when I was on mission in Redcoven as a child. He\rquote 
d been one of the quieter Senior Brothers, if memory served. Unbeatable at the footlist, but not gregarious like some of them had been, not even with his fellows. I think he\rquote d kept to himself a lot. I didn\rquote 
t remember him joining us for any of the gathers around the brazier, or on trips into the city\rquote s market district.
