\par \'93I have an oath to fulfill,\'94 I said, as solidly as I could manage, \'93to my Order, my Brothers, and to God. I may not enjoy the thought of you suffering\'85 of anyone suffering, regardless of their crime\'85
 but the verdict of the mortal Courts in this case will only echo the verdict of God, and God is clear on what punishments should and must be levied against criminals who\rquote ve done what you\rquote ve done. It isn\rquote t for me to question.\'94

\par \'93You\rquote re lying again,\'94 he insisted, shakily. \'93You question }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid8607637 everything}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 
 in that holy tome of yours. I\rquote ve read your journal.\'94
\par My jaw fell open. \'93You read my prayer book?\'94
\par Honestly, of all the things I\rquote d thought the Heart Thief would do with my prayer book, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid8607637 reading}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 
 it had somehow never been one I\rquote d considered.
\par \'93Oh, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid2766769 every}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  page,\'94 he said, emphatically. \'93Why do you think I took it? To spite you?\'94
\par I was, of course, horrifically offended, outraged, I hardly knew how to feel! What was one to think of such a breach of one\rquote s privacy?! Snarling, and grasping for any suitable response, I spat out the first thing that came to mind.
\par \'93Your thoughts?!\'94
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid7829587 No}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 . Why had I asked }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid7829587 that?!}
{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034   }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid7829587 What did their opinion matter?! That wasn\rquote t the point!}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 
\par He blinked up at me, seemingly as stunned by my question as I was.
\par \'93Well?!\'94 I gurgled out in a growl, desperately. To hell with it, I was saddled to the horse, now. We rode.
\par \'93I\'85\'94 his expression softened around the edges, fuzzy with discernable thought. His eyes unfocused for just a moment, and I was struck with the realization that this was the first time I\rquote d ever met him that I\rquote d seen him
 lost for words.
\par His gaze re-focused on mine after another few seconds, and when he spoke again, the uncertainty had bled away to his usual collected, confident tone. 
\par \'93It made me sad,\'94 he said softly, his first words not at all what I\rquote d expected, but when he continued it grew even more strange, \'93to think that you and I will never be friends.\'94
\par \'93What?!\'94 I coughed on my own saliva, ineloquently.
\par He gave the slightest inclination of a smile, tempered by that ever-present fear and wariness since the subject of his crimes had come up. \'93Your writings were thoughtful\'85 intelligent\'85\'94 he paused a moment, then pressed on, \'93\'85 lonely. I fe
lt at times unsettlingly familiar with some of the feelings you profess, and deeply curious to understand you and\'85 your life\'85 better than I can from your writings alone. I think talking to you at length would be fascinating.\'94 He closed his
 eyes a moment. \'93But as I said, we cannot be friends. Or even acquaintances. So, it is for naught.\'94
\par \'93I am not }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid10755715 lonely}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 ,\'94 I insisted, vehemently. \'93I have a family. A Father. Siblings. God.\'94
\par \'93So you do. But it seems to me,\'94 he said, carefully, \'93that you only share your inner thoughts with the page. Or in Confession\'85 and only those which trouble you, or that you feel are wrong. You\'85 wrote as much\'85\'94
\par I swallowed. It was true that I\rquote d used my prayer book primarily as a place to write down the things I didn\rquote t feel I could share or bring myself to speak about with the monks or other boys at the Abbey. Darcy wasn\rquote t entirely correct
, I }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid9387819 had}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  talked about some of them with Father Helstrom, but\'85 I suppose, not very many.
\par It felt so odd, knowing that someone else- someone mortal, someone other than God- knew them, now. Not\'85 terrible, exactly. I wanted to know what someone might }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid15479131 th}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 ink}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  of these things I\rquote d never shared.
\par He\rquote d told me how he felt about }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid15479131 me}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 , I suppose. But I wanted to talk to him, to someone, about\'85
 all of it! About each subject, each wayward musing. I wanted to know if I was just a fool, or if anything that knocked around in my head had merit. 
\par For some reason, I\rquote d always hesitated at the cusp of each of these questions, in asking or speaking with Father Helstrom. There were plenty of other conversations we\rquote d had. But some things, just\'85 it didn\rquote 
t feel right to talk to my Father about them. And there was no one else at home who would be appropriate. That\rquote s why I\rquote d started journaling at all. I hadn\rquote t known what else to do.
\par But Darcy would not, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid14109133 could}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  not be that person, for me. That was impossible. We couldn\rquote 
t really talk about anything at length. Because of who we were.
\par \'93It also saddens me,\'94 he spoke again, after an uncomfortable- and I\rquote d imagine telling- pause had passed between us, \'93that you feel you can only share these doubts and thoughts when they make you guilty enough to give Confession.
 What a terrible association. You should have someone you can talk to candidly, without the stain of shame hanging over you.\'94
\par \'93Shame is a useful trait for self-improvement,\'94 I said, parroting Brother Dolus\rquote s words from many times in the past. \'93You should try it. You could stand to give Confession, I\rquote m sure.\'94
\par His shoulders slumped marginally, then he sighed. \'93There go the walls back up, then.\'94 He finally separated our hands. My knuckles felt chilled from the loss of it. He ran a finger along the tip of my blade, eyeline tracing its path towards his
 heart. \'93Did you read the books }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid3934220 I}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  left you, by any chance?\'94
\par \'93I did,\'94 I affirmed, lifting my chin. \'93Trash, all three of them.\'94
\par \'93Ugh,\'94 he clucked his tongue, \'93maybe it\rquote s best we remain enemies. If you can\rquote t respect a classic piece of literature like \lquote The Admiral\rquote s White Claw\rquote , then you have terrible taste, and we\rquote 
d never get along.\'94 His muzzle twisted up for a moment petulantly, and he pressed, \'93Did you even really }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid9266161 read}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  it? I just don\rquote t know how you could call it \lquote trash\rquote  if you did. You probably just skimmed it- you can\rquote t imbibe the beauty of the prose that way-\'93
\par \'93I read it cover to cover three times,\'94 I asserted, firmly. 
\par \'93You lie yet again!\'94 he exclaimed, his fangs peeking out from beneath his muzzle as he laughed at me.
\par \'93Don\rquote t make that accusation lightly, where I\rquote m concerned,\'94 I fired back. \'93I have no idea why, but you penned notes for me in the margins, as to which scenes were your favorites and why. I\rquote m assuming that\rquote s why the qu
ill and ink was there\'85 and I know they were for }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid8796301 me}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  because you used my bloody name.\'94
 I tapped his chest with the tip of my blade, shoving him an inch. \'93There. Is that proof enough for you?\'94
\par \'93Oh my God,\'94 he full-out laughed, now. \'93You\rquote re telling the truth. And the others two books?\'94
\par \'93Only once, but yes.\'94
\par \'93How on earth did you have the time?\'94 he scoffed. \'93And why read \lquote White Claw\rquote  thrice? If you hated it so\'85\'94
\par \'93I thought there must certainly be something in the texts that would give me a better understanding of who you were,\'94 I explained. \'93A hidden message, an inkling of your methodologies. But it really was all just tripe meant to waste my time, wasn
\rquote t it?\'94
\par \'93You didn\rquote t }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid15747451 need}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  to read them,\'94 he said, concealing his grin only barely by lifting his
 free hand to his muzzle. \'93Let alone }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid810782 three times}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 .\'94
\par \'93It isn\rquote t as though there was very much else for me to do, chasing after you for days on the road,\'94 I groused. \'93Let alone at the pace my mule was setting.\'94
\par \'93You read the books on horseback?\'94 He asked, eyes wide. 
\par \'93Not a horse-\'93
\par \'93That is the most lovely, wholesome thing I\rquote ve ever heard of!\'94 he exclaimed, laughing again. God, his laugh made something twinge in my chest, and I didn\rquote t like ruminating on why that was. Not one bit. It should be }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid8796301 different}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  now that I knew the truth of his identity! 
\par \'93I mean, to read in a carriage is one thing, but you must be a truly voracious reader to do so in the saddle,\'94 he prattled on. \'93Isidor, I feel\'85 just\'85 deprived, that we must have such acr
imony between us. I am only more certain now that we would have been friends, in another life.\'94
\par \'93You chose to be a criminal,\'94 I levied back. \'93The fault is yours. You could have been a decent, respectable member of society. You could have made better choices.\'94
\par \'93Young Sir,\'94 he intoned, \'93becoming a criminal is the only choice I have ever made for myself. You will never make me regret it. Even if you kill me for it.\'94
\par I was about to question him on the lunacy of his statement, when both of his ears swiveled forward and his eyes went wide as full moons, staring directly above me in alarm. He fell away 
quickly to his knees in front of me like I were a tower shield. The fact that I let him betrayed all but instantly the lack of conviction in my sword arm\'85 
because I did not drive the blade home, I merely allowed him to move away from it with a half-hearted chase. But I had very little time to ruminate on that, before a familiar voice swore from above me and something sung past my shoulder, embedding in the 
ground several feet away from me. It had either been an extremely bad shot, or he\rquote d tipped the weapon at the last moment.
\par In a state of shock, and forgetting myself, I craned my neck back and looked above me to an open window, where Malachi was leaning out with his crossbow deployed. I began to say something, when he snapped out, \'93Eyes on her! She\rquote s armed!\'94

\par I stepped back a foot reflexively, bringing my blade and buckler arm up to guard at the same time as I turned my attention back to Darcy.
\par Just in time to catch the tail end of his swift motion, as he lifted a slim flintlock pistol from behind his back, and leveled it at my head.
\par For some reason, with what could have been my last breath, I said his name.
\par His hands were shaking visibly where he held the flintlock, one cupping the other to hold it steady. This wasn\rquote t a bluff. He knew how to shoot. He was just scared.
\par Which wasn\rquote t a good thing, really.
\par \'93Isidor,\'94 he breathed out in shudder. \'93I am very\'85 very sorry for this.\'94
\par There was a clattering overhead, the sound of wood splintering and a muffled grunt of pain from Malachi, as he forced himself out through a too-small window, and then a thud as he presumably landed on the adjacent balcony. I\rquote 
d seen the man leap and move by now\'85 he\rquote d be here in a moment, but by then I\rquote d-
\par I tried to reach towards Darcy with my off-hand, knowing at that point I had very little to lose. And I saw his finger flex on the trigger. 
\par At the last second, his wrist\'85 twitched\'85 and the pistol went off. The sound at point-blank range, and the spark, the smoke\'85 it could have heralded the end, for me. The last thing I\rquote d ever see, smell, hear\'85
\par But when I opened my eyes, I was prone on my back on the stone, very much alive. I hardly remembered falling, but being fired at point-blank by a flintlock will do that to a man. My ears were ringing and I may have hit my head when I\rquote 
d fallen, because everything was spinning and hazy. A pair of big, canine paws thundered past me, and then I\rquote m not certain how long later, returned, joined by the man\rquote s knees and two strong hands scooping me up by the shoulders.
\par I blinked, trying to get my bearings, and vaguely heard my name amongst the ringing din. Things were starting to make more sense. I followed the sound of my name to its source, a canine face, masked in
 dark brown, with two fierce eyes the color of the sky. 
\par \'93Damn, lad,\'94 his words formed up more clearly as he slid one of his palms over the back of my head, and brought it up bloody. \'93You knocked your head good, there. Are you with me?\'94
\par \'93M\rquote I\'85 shot?\'94 I asked, blearily.
\par \'93Not unless it ricocheted,\'94 he growled, settling me back down and beginning to undo my harness, pressing his palms over every part of my body from neck to waist, looking presumably for any more blood. \'93Damned whore shot right past your head, t
hough. Into the wall over there. I\rquote ve no bloody clue how she missed a shot that close. God loves you, Isidor of Ebon Gables. God is on your side\'85\'94
\par \'93Why did you shoot at him?\'94 I asked, my voice sounding odd to me\'85 }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid10306289 everything}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 
 sounded odd to me. My hearing was muddy, like I was underwater.
\par \'93I had a good line of sight to her shoulder before the two of you moved,\'94 he explained. \'93You moved right into my line of fire when she ducked. I could see the bloody pistol tucked into her trousers from up there. Couldn\rquote t take the risk she
\rquote d shoot you before I made it downstairs.\'94
\par \'93I\rquote m sorry, I\'85\'94 I mumbled, choking on my words, the whole of it catching up to me at once. I felt my eyes sting. I would }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid10306289 not}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034  cry in front of this man. \'93I lost him. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid10306289 Again}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 .\'94
\par \'93I don\rquote t fucking care,\'94 he swore. \'93Are you alright? You need to- come on, sit up if you can- I need to see if you were hit by a ricochet, I need to see your back, come on, lad\'85\'94
\par My body wanted nothing more than to continue lying there, chasing off wave after wave of dizziness and crashing exhaustion, as the reality of what had just nearly happened washed over me. But, fighting back nausea and 
dark spots in my vision that threatened to black me out entirely, I willed myself to do as he said, groaning gutturally as I bent slowly upwards.
\par \'93There\'85 there, now,\'94 his voice was still a growl, but it was clear he was trying to imbue it with calm. \'93You\rquote re alright. No blood\'85 except on your head\'85 and we\rquote ll get that seen to.\'94
\par He was holding me by the shoulders, I realized. He\rquote d helped me sit up. For the first time in the half a day since we\rquote d met, I realized the man didn\rquote t seem to keep to the unspoken 
barriers that other senior men in the Order had, my entire life. He had no issue with touching me as familiarly as my brothers did, despite being as good as a stranger to me.
\par Right now, I was exceptionally grateful for that fact.
\par \'93I\rquote ve\'85 failed,\'94 I choked around another heavy swallow. \'93Again.\'94
\par \'93No, Brother Isidor,\'94 he sighed, looping an arm under my armpit, and slowly easing me to my feet, \'93}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f44\fs36\insrsid9663034\charrsid5209665 we}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs36 \ltrch\fcs0 \f44\fs36\insrsid9663034 
 have failed. And I\rquote m your Senior, so I\rquote m all the more to blame. I made the call to take that shot.\'94
\par \'93You had a good reason,\'94 I mumbled.
\par \'93I also failed to run her down,\'94 he said. \'93She scaled the back wall, there\'85 bloody cats. I couldn\rquote t climb it, and by the time I got around, there was no trace of her.\'94
\par \'93You should be out there looking,\'94 I insisted weakly. \'93We should\'85 he must be close.\'94
\par \'93We will find her again,\'94 he said, his voice hard as iron and inviting no argument. \'93You knocked your head on paving stones. We\rquote re taking you to a Physician. Now. I am not losing one of my Brothers over that wench\'85 and did you say 
\lquote him\rquote ?\'94
\par \'93He\rquote s a man,\'94 I explained, simply.
\par He seemed to accept it all but immediately. \'93I knew this profile seemed too clever for a female,\'94 he muttered. \'93I assumed she had help from whatever Pedigree she\rquote s seduced, but this is all beginning to make more sense, now.\'94
\par I sagged and he wrapped his arm lower around my waist, pulling me back upright. \'93Stay with me, lad.\'94
\par \'93God help me. I have had\'85 indecent dreams about her\'85 about him\'85\'94 I muttered, the words spilling out of me like I was at the gates, admitting my sins to the Chorus.
\par He stilled, and I was too far gone to know how he looked in that moment, but he felt rigid. We began to move together again, the city a hazy collage to me. 
\par \'93You don\rquote t tell anyone else that, ever,\'94 Malachi said, at some point. \'93You hear me? Not ever. Not even a Priest.\'94
\par \'93Mnhhhh,\'94 I groaned unintelligibly.
\par \'93Not ever,\'94 he whispered.
